<h2>Feeding Vietnam's aquaculture industry</h2>
<p>Vietnam imported 33,008 MT of Indian fish meal worth USD 40.5 million in calendar 2024, the second-largest destination after China (USD 93.1 million, 70,344 MT) among India's USD 258.0 million total fish meal exports worldwide. The driver is straightforward: Vietnam operates one of the world's largest aquaculture sectors, with farmed shrimp and pangasius (basa) catfish production requiring a steady, quality-consistent supply of protein-rich feed ingredients.</p>

<h2>What Vietnamese feed mills actually specify</h2>
<p>Fish meal buyers, in our experience, ask more precise technical questions than most agricultural commodity buyers, and for good reason — feed formulation is a precision exercise. The parameters that matter:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Protein content</strong> — commonly 60% or higher for premium aquafeed applications, lower grades for other livestock feed use</li>
  <li><strong>Freshness indicators</strong> — total volatile nitrogen (TVN) and histamine levels, which reflect how the raw fish was handled before processing began</li>
  <li><strong>Fat content</strong> — affects both nutritional value and storage stability</li>
  <li><strong>Salt content</strong> — relevant for feed formulation balance</li>
</ul>
<p>Tell us your target protein grade and any freshness thresholds your feed formulation requires, and we'll quote against your actual specification.</p>

<h2>Testing and quality assurance</h2>
<p>Because fish meal enters a feed chain that ultimately reaches food-producing animals, we treat microbiological testing (salmonella in particular) and adulteration checks as standard practice rather than an optional extra. {{ site.inspectionStatement }}</p>

<h2>Why India, specifically, for Vietnam</h2>
<p>Peru remains the world's dominant fish meal exporter by volume, so India isn't competing globally on scale. What India offers Vietnamese buyers specifically is a freight and logistics advantage on this particular route — shorter transit and lower shipping cost than sourcing from South America, which matters for a feed input where landed cost per protein unit is the key economics.</p>

<h2>Packaging and storage</h2>
<p>Standard export packing is 25kg or 50kg PP bags, with bulk options available for larger feed-mill contracts. Fish meal needs dry, cool storage — moisture ingress degrades both quality and shelf life, so packaging and transit handling matter as much as the initial specification.</p>

<h2>A note on consistency between shipments</h2>
<p>Feed formulation is precision work, and a fish meal shipment that varies noticeably from the last one — even within nominal specification tolerances — creates real problems for a mill running standardised feed formulas. We aim for batch-to-batch consistency within your agreed specification, and can supply certificates of analysis per shipment so your own quality team can track variation over time rather than discovering a drift only when finished feed performance changes.</p>
